<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Ystrad Tywi</h1><p class="st-meta">450 CE – 730 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Early Welsh kingdom of the Tywi valley in Carmarthenshire, conquered around 730 by Ceredigion to form the realm of Seisyllwg.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ystrad Tywi, 'the vale of the Tywi', covered the fertile river valley and surrounding uplands of what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmarthenshire">Carmarthenshire</a>, including the old Roman town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmarthen">Moridunum</a>, Carmarthen. After Roman rule ended around 410 it seems to have emerged as a separate territory, sometimes held by the kings of the neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-dyfed">Kingdom of Dyfed</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Very little is known about its rulers or government, and no secure king list survives. The Tywi valley was among the best farmland in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, with major church sites such as Llandeilo Fawr, home of the gospel book now known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lichfield-gospels">Lichfield Gospels</a> before it left Wales.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 730 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seisyll-ap-clydog">Seisyll ap Clydog</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-ceredigion">Kingdom of Ceredigion</a> conquered Ystrad Tywi, forming a larger kingdom named <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-seisyllwg">Seisyllwg</a> after him. This passed in the 10th century to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hywel-dda">Hywel Dda</a> and became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-deheubarth">Deheubarth</a>, whose later princes ruled from Dinefwr in the Tywi valley.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-ystrad-tywi.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Ystrad Tywi" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Richard Law · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hillside_and_bog_above_the_source_of_the_Tywi_-_geograph.org.uk_-_2425396.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>450 CE – 730 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Welsh</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ystrad Tywi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=590&amp;focus=kingdom-of-ystrad-tywi" aria-label="Kingdom of Ystrad Tywi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4584 384 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Ystrad Tywi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4904 640L4910 602"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 602h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4904 640h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4904 640h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=590&amp;focus=kingdom-of-ystrad-tywi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
